  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\langle f, g \\rangle_{\\mathbb{C}} = \\frac{1}{4}\\left( \\|f+g\\|^2 - \\|f-g\\|^2 + i\\,(\\|f-ig\\|^2 - \\|f+ig\\|^2) \\right)\\quad\\text{(Mathlib convention)}",
    "plain": "Complex polarization identity. The slug's stated formula is in physics convention (linear in first argument); Mathlib uses math convention (sesquilinear in first argument). In Mathlib's convention the same physics formula computes ⟪g,f⟫ = conj⟪f,g⟫, NOT ⟪f,g⟫. The corrected Mathlib-convention identity has a sign flip on the imaginary correction.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Polarization is the standard mechanism for recovering an inner product from a norm.",
      "Together with the parallelogram law it characterizes inner-product spaces (Jordan–von Neumann 1935).",
      "Underlies operator polarization for self-adjointness: T self-adjoint ⇔ Q(z) := ⟪Tz,z⟫ is real-valued.",
      "Convention mismatch is a recurring pitfall when porting proofs from physics literature to Mathlib."
    ]
  },
